From: RagnarokMac <noreply@ragnarokmac>
Subject: [PATCH] Configurable shared directional movement (client API 1)

Ragnarok has no directional walk packet, only "walk to this cell", so held
keys are translated into a repeated destination a few cells ahead - the same
approach the on-screen joystick already uses. The vector is rotated by the
camera so "up" means up the screen, not up the map.

Implemented by patches/client/, scripts/patch-client-controls.py and the
bundled mods/wasd-movement adapter. The old unconditional KeyboardMove hook
is removed. Keyboard and touch share intent ownership, bounded pathfinding
and native movement packets. Map/network/GUI hooks provide lifecycle and
component notifications without exposing engine globals to plugins.

Q and E turn the camera while held, clamped to the same limits the mouse
drag obeys -- indoor maps allow only a narrow window, so a key press there
turns as far as it can and stops. Space attacks the nearest living monster,
walking into range first when needed; it sends RO's continuous attack, so the
server keeps swinging until the target falls and nothing loops client-side.
Skills stay on roBrowser's own shortcut bar.

The Controls dialog provides per-browser enablement, physical-key rebinding,
arrows, turning, attack and movement/battle-shortcut priority. Text entry, IME, shortcut capture,
modals, blur and hidden pages cancel movement. A fresh physical press is
required after cancellation; OS key repeat cannot revive stale intent.

Tests: tests/client-extensions.test.cjs and tests/e2e/controls.spec.cjs.
The latter requires a real disposable rAthena world and owned Rust server.
